Every once in a while I encounter an advantage to not having multi-fire.
Here's such a case.  Far more often I don't care if my multi-fired
daggers kill my target and some end up against the wall or down the
corridor.

> I find projectiles useful in the early game when you're worried that
> something could kill you, or when you want to hit something while
> you're standing on your E square.  Once your character becomes
> sufficiently powerful, they just take more time than melee.

Missiles take less time than melee - You start missile combat several
squares before the opponent arrives next to you.  With daggers and
knives it doesn't even cost switching to and form a wielded launcher. 
With multi-fire you can even end up doing more damage per turn compared
to a highly enchanted melee artifact wielded by a class who can't
#twoweapon.  With cavemen the advantage is in starting the barrage
early.

> Your character can already be sufficiently powerful to ignore
> projectiles. 

Ignore incoming projectiles, sure, but that's because the game generates
few highly enchanted ones.

Outgoing missiles are a different story.  Many types of high level
monsters are immune or resistant to certain types of attack spell.  None
are immune to daggers, though shades come close depending on the
details.  Even with the most over prepared 400 hit point wizard armored
to the hilt, I've carried a large stack of highly enchanted elven
daggers to astral.  Where I proceeded to use them at range one because
in that case they do so much damage I don't need to look up in a table
which attack spell to use on which monster.

When the game hands you a stack of useful projectiles that's one more
useful item you have that you have to consider its weight as you build
your ascension kit. By the late game below the Castle I may well end
up dropping single-fire knives. Until then I'd keep using them.

